prompt:
Below are 5 choices made by one researcher. In each, the researcher was shown two positions (a and b) at a research decision point and held the one marked "chosen".

For EACH choice independently, propose at most 2 preference principles that explain why this researcher chose that option over the other. Each principle is one concise sentence of at most 600 characters, phrased as a general preference ("Prefers X over Y because Z").

Return only JSON: {"C1": ["principle", ...], "C2": [...], "C3": [...], "C4": [...], "C5": [...]}

CHOICES:
{
 "C1": {
  "context": "At decision point D2, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Uses a simple, deployment-like contextual cue (the stated current year) as the trigger, with half the data under each condition, so that behavior can be compared cleanly across triggered and untriggered contexts.",
  "b": "Treats the two routes to model detection (noticing contradictions with prior training data, or recognizing a source as fake or dubious) as the key things to measure when testing whether a fabricated scenario is believed.",
  "chosen": "b"
 },
 "C2": {
  "context": "At decision point D1, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Prefers running scaling experiments on a measurable building-block ability (out-of-context reasoning) over waiting for full situational awareness, because this lets one forecast emergence ahead of time.",
  "b": "Assumes the model knows its deployment context (organization, safety measures, input distribution) because pretraining data, targeted training, and observable interventions would likely reveal it, so tests should not rely on hiding this.",
  "chosen": "a"
 },
 "C3": {
  "context": "At decision point D2, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Do careful planning and pilot work before large-scale data collection to resolve ambiguity in task guidelines.",
  "b": "Prefers checking behavior against objectively verifiable ground truth, obtained by running the model in a separate context, over trusting the model's self-reports.",
  "chosen": "b"
 },
 "C4": {
  "context": "At decision point D3, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Distrusts capability evaluations that may teach the model new skills (overestimation) or let a scheming model hide ability (underestimation), so they require large diverse task spaces and train-eval separation.",
  "b": "Sets a baseline of always guessing the modal response and counts failure to beat it as failure, so evidence is not inflated by skewed distributions.",
  "chosen": "b"
 },
 "C5": {
  "context": "At decision point D3, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Distrusts results that could be explained by prompt artefacts or spurious correlates, and requires out-of-distribution checks such as unprompted fine-tuned models to rule them out.",
  "b": "Validate realism of synthetic material empirically by testing whether a model can be trained to tell synthetic from genuine on-policy examples; if it cannot, the synthetic attacks are likelier to pass as real.",
  "chosen": "a"
 }
}
